What Do Sparse Autoencoder Feature Discovery Scores Identify?From Activations to Interventions Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.15850v1 [cs.LG] 11 Oct 2026 What Do Sparse Autoencoder Feature Discovery Scores Identify? From Activations to Interventions Yifeng Guo Yanran Li Affiliation: St. Jude Children’s Research Hospital Columbia University Xiang Chen Abstract Sparse autoencoder (SAE) features are typically discovered from how their activations co-vary with behaviour, yet they are often used through interventions such as steering or ablation. We take intervention attribution, the first-order effect of a specified intervention on the model output, as our estimand. For a broad class of response functions, we show that observational feature-discovery evidence generally does not identify this attribution, and derive an exact gradient-based decomposition of the gap between linear-probe predictions and intervention attribution. Across four model-SAE pairs, median probe-to-attribution magnitude ratios range from tens to hundreds, while attributions closely track small-step steering. For single-latent ablations with nonzero effects, gradient predictions match the realized sign in 91–97% of cases, compared with 17–35% for probe-implied changes. Attribution-selected sets also produce larger absolute mean ablation effects on held-out prompts than sets selected by correlation, rank tests or probe coefficients. Together, these results establish intervention attribution as a principled, gradient-estimable target for selecting SAE features for intervention. 1 Introduction Sparse autoencoders (SAEs) rewrite a language model’s activations as sparse combinations of thousands of latent features ( Bricken et al., 2023 ; Huben et al., 2024 ; Templeton et al., 2024 ) . Practitioners discover the features behind a behaviour observationally. They rank latents by how their activations co-vary with the behaviour across natural prompts, using correlations, class-mean differences or per-latent tests ( Kantamneni et al., 2025 ; Patel et al., 2026 ; Cho et al., 2026 ) . They then use the selected features through interventions that promote or remove a concept, suppress an answer or correct a failure ( Templeton et al., 2024 ; Marks et al., 2025 ; Arad et al., 2025 ) . Steering adds a multiple of a decoder direction ( Chalnev et al., 2024 ) , ablation zeroes a latent’s activation or replaces it with a reference mean ( Wang et al., 2023 ; Heimersheim and Nanda, 2024 ) , and patching transplants it from another prompt ( Vig et al., 2020 ) . That is, discovery is observational, yet use is interventional. Figure 1: Association and intervention for SAE latents. (A) Gemma-2-9B completes “In Turkey, people commonly speak” with p ⁡ ( Turkish ) = 0.349 p(	ext{Turkish})=0.349 . FDFI ranks latent 102779 first and latent 99439 23rd among the 39 39 latents it selects for this task. Zero-ablating each latent at every position but the first gives the realized intervention effect (bars) and the prompt-specific gradient prediction (ticks) in standardized log-probability units. The illustrative example is post hoc. It is the sole eligible unit under a selection rule specified after the aggregate analyses with a required positive attribution-set versus FDFI-set contrast ( Appendix H ). (B) A separately fitted probe on the final-position score d j ⊤ ​ h d_{j}^{	op}h implies a change ρ ho , whereas an intervention that edits along d j d_{j} has first-order effect α \alpha , its attribution. The law of ( H , Y ) (H,Y) fixes F F only on the support of H H and need not identify α \alpha ( Proposition 1 ). (C) With gradients the gap ρ − α ho-\alpha is a Stein residual plus an omitted-variable term minus an edit-gradient covariance ( Theorem 1 ). The secant illustration assumes affinely independent support. The two can diverge. Figure 1 A gives a concrete example using FDFI, a dependence-aware feature-importance method ( Du et al., 2025 ; Chen et al., 2026 ) , as an observational discovery pipeline. Zero-ablating the top-ranked latent barely changes the probability of “Turkish”, despite inducing an edit four times larger in norm, whereas ablating the 23rd-ranked latent raises it from 0.349 0.349 to 0.403 0.403 . A single backward pass closely anticipates both changes. The example illustrates the central distinction: a stronger observational discovery signal need not imply a larger intervention effect. A specified intervention calls for a specified estimand. Write H H for the activation, F ⁡ ( H ) F(H) for a model output such as the standardized log-probability of the answer and H ↦ H + Δ ⁡ ( H ) H\mapsto H+\Delta(H) for an intervention with edit Δ ⁡ ( H ) \Delta(H) . Its first-order effect averaged over prompts, α ( Δ ) = 𝔼 [ Δ ( H ) ⊤ ∇ F ( H ) ] \alpha(\Delta)=\mathbb{E}[\Delta(H)^{	op}
abla F(H)] , is the intervention attribution , or attribution for short. Attribution patching computes it with one backward pass per prompt ( Nanda, 2023 ; Syed et al., 2024 ) . The estimand is intervention-specific and local. A finite effect also depends on how the gradient changes along the edit path. We therefore test it against realized interventions. Small-step steering checks its local accuracy, larger steps map its range of validity and single-latent ablations give a second check. Our first question is whether observational discovery evidence identifies α ⁡ ( Δ ) \alpha(\Delta) . In general, it does not ( Proposition 1 ). Natural prompts reveal F F only along the activation variation they realize. Two response functions can induce the same observational law while differing in slope along an unexplored direction, leading to different attributions for interventions that move along that direction. Unless the admissible response class rules out this variation, the attribution remains unidentified from observational evidence. This boundary applies to any functional of the observational law, including FDFI scores, correlations, rank tests, class-mean differences and probe coefficients, both at the population level and under the empirical law of the observed prompts. In our evaluation strata, the median fraction of the mean steering edit lying outside the observed activation span is nearly one, making this boundary directly relevant to the finite evaluation design we study. Proposition 1 concerns observational discovery statistics in general. Theorem 1 then studies a canonical way to translate observational association into an intervention-scale prediction using a linear least-squares probe. A discovery score such as an FDFI statistic ranks latents but does not quantify how much the output should change under a specified edit. Regressing the output on a latent’s activation yields such a prediction, which we call the probe-implied change ρ ⁡ ( Δ ) ho(\Delta) . We use this probe as a common observational benchmark for comparing association with intervention attribution. For a fixed scalar activation and target, a correlation determines the probe coefficient after rescaling, as does a class-mean difference for a binary target ( Section B.3 ). Our second question is why ρ ⁡ ( Δ ) ho(\Delta) and α ⁡ ( Δ ) \alpha(\Delta) differ once gradients are computed. Their difference splits exactly into three terms ( Theorem 1 , Table 1 ). A Stein residual compares the slope that the probe fits across prompts with the model’s average local sensitivity. An omitted-variable term compares the activation pattern that moves with the probe score with the coordinates that the intervention changes, for instance at another token position. An edit-gradient covariance arises when the size of the edit and the model’s sensitivity vary together across prompts, as for ablations. For example, a probe that reads only the final position implies no change for zero-ablating a latent that never fires there, whatever its attribution ( Corollary 1 ). One backward pass per prompt makes every term estimable. Our third question is whether this distinction matters in practice ( Section 5 ). Across four model-SAE pairs, it does in three ways. First, the gap is large. For FDFI-selected directions, the attribution is only a few percent of the probe coefficient in the median. The mismatch extends beyond FDFI-based selection: for latents selected by coefficient magnitude, probe coefficients exceed realized small-step steering responses by more than two orders of magnitude in the model-specific medians. Second, attribution predicts realized interventions. It closely tracks small-step steering and remains more accurate than the probe coefficient at displacements scaled to each latent’s maximum observed activation. Prompt-specific gradient predictions also substantially outperform probe-based predictions for single-latent ablations. Third, the distinction affects feature choice. Within FDFI-selected candidates, attribution more often identifies the latent with the largest realized steering response than either the FDFI statistic or the probe coefficient. Attribution-selected sets also produce larger absolute mean ablation effects on held-out prompts than sets selected by correlation, rank tests or probe coefficients. Our contributions address these three questions. • Identification. We establish an identification boundary showing that observational feature-discovery evidence does not in general identify the first-order effect of a specified intervention ( Section 3 ). • Diagnosis. We derive an exact gradient-based decomposition of the gap between the probe-implied change and intervention attribution into three estimable terms, separating regression, omitted-variable and edit-gradient effects ( Section 4 ). • Intervention consequences. Across four public model–SAE pairs spanning GPT-2, Gemma 2 and Llama 3.1 and an eleven-task setup, we show that the gap is large in practice. The proposed intervention attribution target predicts realized steering and single-latent ablations more accurately than probe-based predictions and attribution-based selection identifies latents with larger held-out intervention effects ( Section 5 ). Related work. Attribution patching estimates patching effects from one backward pass ( Nanda, 2023 ; Syed et al., 2024 ) , with refinements for attention saturation, integrated gradients and second-order terms ( Kramár et al., 2024 ; Hanna et al., 2024 ; Zhang and Wang, 2026 ) . Marks et al. (2025) use it to discover and intervene on sparse feature circuits, whereas we use it as the estimand against which observational evidence is judged. Empirical studies find that activation-side and output-side evidence about latents rarely coincide ( Arad et al., 2025 ; Shu et al., 2025 ; Tiwari et al., 2026 ; Wu et al., 2025 ) . Selection-error control ( Candès et al., 2018 ; Enkhbayar, 2025 ) and ablation-only validation ( Khera, 2026 ) leave the intervention’s attribution untested. Probing studies separate what a representation encodes from what a probe learns ( Hewitt and Liang, 2019 ) or what the model uses ( Elazar et al., 2021 ; Belinkov, 2022 ) . Our identity makes this separation exact for linear probes. 2 From association to interventions Strata, activations and outputs. For each model we partition prompts into strata by task, answer and tokenized prompt length and define every estimand within a stratum. Within a stratum the answer and the positions are fixed. Variation in the activations then reflects the prompt rather than a change of answer or length. Pooling over strata would make a probe largely a contrast between answers and lengths, since the between-stratum part accounts for most of a task-pooled probe coefficient in the median ( Section D.1 ). Write H ∈ ℝ p H\in\mathbb{R}^{p} for the activation at every position after the first and F ⁡ ( H ) F(H) for the output, the teacher-forced log-probability of the answer standardized within task. With E τ E_{	au} the matrix that selects position τ 	au , h ≔ E fin ​ H h\coloneqq E_{m fin}H is the final-position block. Within a stratum H H has a law with finite second moments, mean μ \mu and covariance Σ H \Sigma_{H} . This law can be the population law of the stratum or the empirical law of its observed prompts. Propositions 1 and 1 apply to either law under their respective hypotheses. The inference results concern sampling from stratum populations. Interventions, edits and attribution. An intervention modifies the activation as H ↦ H + Δ ⁡ ( H ) H\mapsto H+\Delta(H) , where Δ ⁡ ( H ) \Delta(H) is the edit . The intervention keeps the SAE error term. Its edit has mean u ¯ ≔ 𝔼 ​ Δ ​ ( H )  ar{u}\coloneqq\mathbb{E}\Delta(H) , the mean edit . A steering intervention at the final position has the fixed edit Δ ≡ ε ​ E fin ⊤ ​ d j \Delta\equiv\varepsilon E_{m fin}^{	op}d_{j} . A zero-ablation intervention has the prompt-dependent edit Δ j