 indirect object identification) and arithmetic (simple addition and two-digit subtraction) ( Section D.1 ). The antonym task, with no evaluation stratum of at least three prompts, enters only the two-prompt ablation sensitivity analysis and the held-out set-ablation comparison. Unless stated otherwise, quantities are estimated on the held-out evaluation split within strata of at least three prompts, in within-task standard deviations of the log-probability ( Section D.1 ). Stratum-level t t intervals use a many-strata normal approximation ( Theorem C.1 ) and in simulation at the observed stratum sizes cover near the nominal level for the ablation terms and below it for the Stein residual ( Section C.2 ). Other intervals are percentile bootstrap intervals. The fixed-direction analysis, the coefficient-selected steering and the single-latent ablations are prespecified. The maximum-activation steering and the set-ablation comparison of selection rules are post hoc, specified after the aggregate results and before evaluation. The probe-to-ablation breakdown, the averaged-summary analysis, the ranking comparison and the worked example are post hoc ( Section D.2 ). Because steering uses step sizes scaled on the evaluation split, its comparisons are descriptive. The results answer three questions. How large is the gap? Does the attribution predict realized interventions? Does the distinction change which latents one would choose? Table 2: Main results on held-out prompts. Columns Pairs through | 𝖼 / β | |\mathsf{c}/ eta| use FDFI-selected directions under unit steering. Ratios are medians over pairs, with intervals that resample strata within task. Column < 0.1 <0.1 is the share with | α / β | < 0.1 |\alpha/ eta|<0.1 and ρ ≈ 0 ho\approx 0 is the post hoc share of single-latent zero-ablations with | ρ | ≤ 10 − 3 < | α | |ho|\leq 10^{-3}<|\alpha| . Steering is descriptive, on coefficient-selected directions ( 90 90 , 100 100 , 100 100 and 90 90 pairs), with T T the centra