a on symmetric-log axes under zero-ablation of the 756 756 FDFI-selected pairs. Shading marks opposite signs and negligible predictions ( | ρ | < 10 − 3 |ho|<10^{-3} ). Dashed diagonals mark equality. (C) Shares of the summed absolute terms of ( 5 ) for the final-position probe, with the omitted-variable (OV) term split by position and zero- and mean-ablation as upper and lower bars for each model. Panel A is descriptive. Panels B and C are post hoc and descriptive ( Sections E.2 and E.4 ). Size of the gap. For FDFI-selected directions under unit steering, the median ratio of attribution to probe-coefficient magnitude is 2.8 2.8 to 4.6 % 4.6\% across models, with the ratio below 10% for most pairs ( Table 2 ). This mismatch is not specific to FDFI. When we instead select 10 active latents per task by selection-split coefficient magnitude, their coefficients exceed realized small-step steering quotients by more than two orders of magnitude in the model-specific medians. The decomposition points to why: under both selections, the Stein residual and omitted-variable term are each comparable in magnitude to the coefficient. The discrepancy also persists beyond the final-position probe. In a post hoc descriptive analysis, probes on position-averaged decoder scores and FDFI’s averaged-code input remove most of the blindness to unread positions yet still overstate the attribution in aggregate ( Section E.5 ). Finally, sampling-noise analyses and prespecified robustness variants support the same qualitative conclusion ( Sections E.3 and E.1 ). Where the gap arises. Ablation interventions reveal how the terms in Corollary 1 arise in SAE pipelines. On GPT-2 and the Gemma models, about a third of FDFI-selected pairs have a non-negligible attribution but a zero or negligible probe-implied change ( Figure 2 B, Table 2 ). Almost all of these latents never fire at the final position, which is the only position read by the probe. For these pairs, the gap arises at unread positions and is dominated by the omitted-variable term, with the edit-gradient covariance accounting for much of the remainder ( Figure 2 C, Section E.4 ). More broadly, under zero-ablation, the covariance term C j C_{j} exceeds the mean-activation term in magnitude for a substantial share of selected pairs ( Section F.1 ). Predicting realized interventions. We first test whether attribution predicts realized interventions in the local regime. For coefficient-selected directions, we steer held-out prompts by a quarter of a within-stratum standard deviation of the latent score. The unit-direction attribution closely matches the realized central difference quotient T T and is closer than the probe coefficient for every pair ( Figure 2 A, Table 2 , Section E.2 ). We next examine how far this first-order approximation remains accurate by increasing the steering displacement for FDFI-selected directions. At a displacement scaled to each latent’s maximum observed activation, attribution agrees in sign with T T for nearly all pairs and remains closer than the coefficient for almost all. At five times this scale, the approximation breaks down for most pairs, with T T differing from the attribution by more than half its magnitude. Single-latent ablations provide an independent check. Prompt-specific gradient predictions have lower mean absolute error than probe-based predictions on every model and substantially higher sign agreement among ablations with nonzero realized effects ( Table 2 , Section F.2 ). Choosing latents for intervention. The gap affects feature choice as well as effect prediction. Within FDFI-selected candidates, attribution more often ranks first the latent with the largest realized steering response than either the FDFI statistic or the probe coefficient ( Section G.1 ). This agreement is nearly universal at small displacements and remains strong at a displacement scaled to the latent’s maximum observed activation, before weakening as the first-order approximation breaks down at larger scales. We then ask whether this advantage carries over to held-out feature selection. Using training folds to select up to 10 latents per task, attribution-selected sets produce larger absolute mean ablation effects on held-out prompts than correlation-, rank-test- or coefficient-selected sets in every model-task unit ( Section G.2 ). These contrasts remain positive after normalization by edit norm and their two-stage intervals exclude zero. Attribution-selected sets also exceed class-mean-difference sets in most units. Compared with FDFI selection, the contrast is positive in most units and in three of four models. 6 Discussion Observational discovery and intervention attribution answer different questions. Empirical studies report that activation-side evidence about latents need not predict their effects under intervention ( Arad et al., 2025 ; Tiwari et al., 2026 ) . We formalize this separation with an intervention-specific estimand, an identification boundary within the response class of Proposition 1 and an exact decomposition ( Theorem 1 ). In the evaluated pipelines the gap changes which latents one would choose. Discovery statistics remain useful for finding candidate features, whereas claims about the consequence of a specified intervention rest on evidence about that intervention. In a white-box model one backward pass per prompt computes the empirical attribution. For each such intervention one can report its attribution with a held-out interval, the terms of ( 5 ) and the share of the mean edit outside the observed span. Scope and extensions. The attribution is the first-order effect of a specified intervention. Whether a latent is the sole mechanism behind a behaviour or necessary or sufficient for it is a broader mechanistic question. The identity holds for any layer, dictionary, linear readout and intervention that keeps the SAE error term. Our experiments cover four model-SAE pairs at one layer each and eleven tasks, with few selected pairs for Llama-3.1-8B, teacher-forced outputs and steering up to five maximum activations. Two directions follow. For inference, the stratum-level construction targets a fixed-weight population attribution under independent sampling across strata ( Appendix C ). Inference over template clusters would relax that independence. Anytime-valid confidence sequences under adaptive intervention sampling ( Asiaee, 2026 ) suggest a route to inference for adaptively chosen interventions. For finite effects, the intervention must also be evaluated at the scale of interest. Downstream nonlinearities can make first-order approximations inaccurate ( Zhang and Wang, 2026 ) , motivating identification and inference beyond local attribution. References Anders et al. (2020) Christopher Anders, Plamen Pasliev, Ann-Kathrin Dombrowski, Klaus-Robert Müller, and Pan Kessel. Fairwashing explanations with off-manifold detergent. In Proceedings of the 37th International Conference on Machine Learning , volume 119 of Proceedings of Machine Learning Research , pages 314–323. PMLR, 2020. URL https://proceedings.mlr.press/v119/anders20a.html . Arad et al. (2025) Dana Arad, Aaron Mueller, and Yonatan Belinkov. SAEs are good for steering – if you select the right features. In Proceedings of the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing , pages 10241–10259, 2025. doi: 10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.519 . Asiaee (2026) Amir Asiaee. Certified interventional fidelity: Anytime-valid, adaptive evaluation of causal claims in mechanistic interpretability. In Proceedings of the 42nd Conference on Uncertainty in Artificial Intelligence , volume 337 of Proceedings of Machine Learning Research , pages 210–227. PMLR, 2026. URL https://proceedings.mlr.press/v337/asiaee26d.html . Belinkov (2022) Yonatan Belinkov. Probing classifiers: Promises, shortcomings, and advances. Computational Linguistics , 48(1):207–219, 2022. doi: 10.1162/coli_a_00422 . Benjamini and Hochberg (1995) Yo